WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.863
RTIC security does not stop at task and
resource design.

2
00:00:02.943 --> 00:00:05.808
Embedded firmware may store device
identity, keys, certificates,

3
00:00:05.888 --> 00:00:09.508
pairing data, calibration values,
configuration secrets, or service credentials.

4
00:00:09.588 --> 00:00:12.202
These values must be handled as part of
the product lifecycle.

5
00:00:12.282 --> 00:00:15.550
Secrets should not appear in source code,
debug output, logs, crash reports,

6
00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.541
broad build artifacts, or diagnostic
dumps.

7
00:00:17.621 --> 00:00:20.436
Rust ownership can help make sensitive
data flow easier to review,

8
00:00:20.516 --> 00:00:22.828
but storage, provisioning, redaction,
replacement,

9
00:00:22.908 --> 00:00:25.171
and retirement policies still need
explicit design.

10
00:00:25.251 --> 00:00:28.820
Firmware integrity and update behavior
should be defined before field deployment.

11
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.067
Versioning, authenticity checks, integrity
checks, recovery behavior,

12
00:00:32.147 --> 00:00:35.817
rollback policy, compatibility, and
release evidence all affect whether devices can

13
00:00:35.897 --> 00:00:37.255
be maintained safely over time.

14
00:00:37.335 --> 00:00:41.055
Release hardening also includes separating
development assumptions from production

15
00:00:41.135 --> 00:00:41.738
assumptions.

16
00:00:41.818 --> 00:00:45.086
A setting that is useful during bench
testing may be inappropriate in shipped

17
00:00:45.166 --> 00:00:45.619
firmware.

18
00:00:45.699 --> 00:00:48.866
Teams should identify which diagnostics,
features, keys, symbols, logs,

19
00:00:48.946 --> 00:00:51.761
and debug behaviors are acceptable in each
stage of the lifecycle.

20
00:00:51.841 --> 00:00:55.461
Debug and maintenance interfaces should be
controlled according to product risk and

21
00:00:55.541 --> 00:00:56.395
operational needs.

22
00:00:56.475 --> 00:01:00.095
Serviceability is important, but debug
capabilities should not remain broader than

23
00:01:00.175 --> 00:01:01.231
the threat model allows.

24
00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:04.880
Maintenance access, diagnostics, and
manufacturing modes should have documented

25
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:05.312
policy.

26
00:01:05.392 --> 00:01:07.956
Dependencies, Cargo features, build
scripts, toolchains,

27
00:01:08.036 --> 00:01:11.053
linker configuration, generated code,
firmware artifacts, symbols,

28
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:13.546
signing materials, and release logs all
deserve review.

29
00:01:13.626 --> 00:01:16.893
Release hardening is the process of making
production behavior repeatable,

30
00:01:16.973 --> 00:01:18.180
documented, and auditable.
